PART 3 -- VERDICTS
====================================================================================================
  [PASS] True
         measured: THE FORMATION-EPOCH LADDER z*(sigma), z*+1 = m sigma^2/(k_B T_0): the equilibrium FREEZES at z*(sigma) -- one first-order transition (G132), every rung placed by its own virial temperature against the cooling CMB.  RUNGS @ 5 keV: dSph class 9-20 km/s -> T_b = 0.05-0.26 K < T_CMB(0) -> NO freeze epoch (z* < 0, past-cutoff; floor sigma_min = 65.0 km/s at z* = 0); galaxy 119.2 -> T_b = 9.17 K -> z* = 2.37 = COSMIC NOON (G132 band 2.37-2.49); group 250 -> z* = 13.8 (EoR); CLUSTER class 600-1000 -> T_b = 232-635 K -> z* = 84-232 = THE DARK AGES (G168's class 155-265 at m = 5.7 keV; z*(841, 5 keV) = 166.5 reproduces the parent's z ~ 190 anchor).  THE BRIEF'S '10^4 K-class' CORRECTED: the cluster-class T_b is 2.3e2-6.4e2 K (10^2-10^3 K class), and the z* = 155-265 band corresponds to T_b = 425-725 K -- the placement stands.  TWO PHANTOMS, TWO EPOCHS: the galaxy phantom froze at cosmic noon (z 2.4), the cluster-scale equilibrium would have frozen in the dark ages (z 84-265) -- ~2 orders of magnitude in z from the same law.
  [PASS] True
         measured: THE CONSTANCY TEST (committed flags, n = 12): f_ph(420 kpc) is FLAT across the disturbed/relaxed split.  [floor-A] disturbed (flat-T/NCC-class) median 0.257 vs relaxed (cool-core) median 0.300, Mann-Whitney p = 0.39 (all three conventions p > 0.05); Spearman rho(f_ph, T-flatness) = -0.203 (p = 0.53) and rho(f_ph, profile slope) = -0.175 (p = 0.59): no trend with either assembly indicator; the split explains < 20% of the f_ph variance (within/total = 1.04).  THE ZW1215-TYPE CASE: the ONE cluster with fully-documented disturbance flags (Lagana+19 NCC-disturbed, Lovisari+17 'M', flat official T(r), Eckert+19 lowest f_gas) has f_ph = 0.241, 0.66 sigma from the median -- ordinary, not an outlier.  The two Eckert+19 'below-universal-f_gas' clusters (0.345 median) do not differ from the other 10 (0.288).  VERDICT: the phantom fraction is flat across the disturbed/relaxed split at the committed precision -- the first execution of the constancy test PASSES, with the honest limits: n = 12, one fully flagged cluster, floor-A convention spread 0.15-0.45.
  [PASS] True
         measured: THE HONEST STATEMENT: the DARK-AGES PHANTOM is a TESTABLE RELIC PREDICTION -- the cluster phantom-mass fraction is IMMUNE TO THE CLUSTER'S ASSEMBLY HISTORY.  (1) WHY: the equilibrium froze at z ~ 140-265 (the ladder, V1); since then nothing has touched it -- the free dust is collisionless (G093: v_th < 0.05 km/s; clusters sit (cH0/a0)^2 = 49 above the EFE line -> the free phase) and the phantom is static (G103: relaxation 70-76 dex above Hubble, field-pinned).  Clusters assembled at z = 0-2, LONG after the freeze: the phantom carries no memory of it.  (2) THE TEST EXECUTED: f_ph(420) is flat across the disturbed/relaxed split (V2: p > 0.05 in all conventions, no trend, ZW1215 ordinary) -- 3/3 checks PASS on the committed registers.  (3) THE HONEST LIMITS: (a) n = 12 and ONE fully-flagged cluster -- the constancy claim is executed, not saturated; the discriminating future sample is disturbed/relaxed pairs at fixed M500 with WL mass profiles (the ZW1215 saga shows the HSE footing itself can be the biased one); (b) the floor-A phantom share (0.15-0.45 at 420 kpc) is a CONVENTION -- the equipartition floor of the law's A/r^2 phantom against the observed residual, not a direct measurement of an equilibrium phase; (c) the TENSION, stated: G093/G168 read clusters as the FREE phase (no equilibrium forms) -- the cluster-class rung is the 'equilibrated sector at cluster density' hypothetical; the constancy test is therefore testing the phantom FRACTION the law would place, and its assembly-independence is the relic signature; if future data DID show f_ph tracking assembly (a trend with the disturbance flags), the relic picture would DIE -- this is the testable content.  YES: a testable relic prediction, with the kill condition named.

